{
  "dom_id": "step-q33493",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\n \nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}[-2t⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩+({t}^{2}+2)⟨0,\\,2,\\,2t⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4t,\\,-2{t}^{2}+4,\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{((2)^{2}+2)^{2}}⟨4(2),\\,-2(2)^{2}+4,\\,4(2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}⟨8,-4,8⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{9}‖⟨2,-1,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{9}⟨2,-1,2⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2)\\cdot T(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
